.TP
lazy scan
Scanned where it is. Browsing reads a buffer of rows; queries, sorting and analysis may read the whole input
.TP
decompressed copy
Decompressed whole into a temporary file of the same format in the temp directory (\fB\-\-temp\-dir\fR), which is then scanned lazily. The file is removed on quit (temporary files)
.TP
converted to Arrow
Read through whole into a temporary Arrow IPC file in the temp directory, which is then scanned lazily. Removed on quit, like a decompressed copy; nothing is cached between sessions
.TP
in memory
Read whole into memory before the table appears. Past \fBmemory_warning\fR in \fB[read]\fR (\fB"1GiB"\fR by default; 0 never asks), datui asks first: \fBbig.json: JSON reads 2.0 GiB into memory\fR. A model file's table is one row per tensor, from the header, so it is small however large the model, and is never asked about; a MIDI file is at most 64 MiB (\fBmidi_bytes\fR in \fB[limits]\fR)
.IP \(bu 4
\fBCompressed\fR is a \fB.gz\fR, \fB.zst\fR, \fB.bz2\fR or \fB.xz\fR file; \fBno\fR means it does not open. \fB\-c read.\:decompress_in_memory=true\fR reads compressed CSV, TSV, PSV and text in memory instead.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBHTTP(S)\fR is one file at an \fBhttp://\fR or \fBhttps://\fR URL. \fBdownloaded\fR copies it to the temp directory first, then reads it as \fBRead\fR says. A model file's header is fetched by range; a server that sends no ranges gets the download question.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBIn a bucket\fR is one S3, GCS or Azure object. \fBin place\fR reads only what is needed with ranged requests: a Parquet or Arrow IPC file's footer and the rows shown, or a model file's header. \fBdownloaded\fR copies the object to the temp directory first, after asking, then reads it as \fBRead\fR says; an Arrow stream is converted as it downloads, with no copy of the stream kept.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBBucket prefix\fR is a prefix or glob read as one table. Only Parquet reads hive partitions; the model files directly under a prefix are read by their headers. An Arrow prefix scans its IPC files in place and downloads its streams, one split of a Hugging Face cache as on disk; a glob of Arrow reads IPC files only. A prefix marked \fBno\fR opens one object at a time from the cloud source.
.IP \(bu 4
Standard input is written to a temporary file first, then read as \fBRead\fR says.
.PP
The home screen marks a file row that is not read lazily where it is: \fBdecompresses\fR, \fBconverts\fR, \fBin memory\fR or \fBdownloads\fR. The details pane and the Info panel's Resources tab say how it is read.
.SH "DETECTED BY CONTENT"
The format comes from the first bytes, unless \fB\-\-format\fR or \fB\-\-compression\fR names it:
.TP
Parquet, Arrow IPC or Avro magic number, or an Arrow IPC stream's schema message
that format
.TP
\fBSQLite format 3\fR
SQLite; a database of several tables needs \fB\-\-table\fR
.TP
gzip, zstd, bzip2 or xz magic number
decompressed, then read by what is inside, as below: a text format, CSV or TSV, or lines
.TP
\fB{\fR, the first line an object with \fB__CURSOR\fR and \fB__REALTIME_TIMESTAMP\fR
systemd journal
.TP
\fB[\fR, then JSON
JSON
.TP
\fB{\fR, the first line a whole object
NDJSON
.TP
\fB{\fR, the object open past the first line, JSON so far
JSON
